    {
      "id": "KpTan9qew3f4fciVAP0WO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -137.56572703828783,
      "y": 2512.6897929669194,
      "width": 2792.51513671875,
      "height": 2770.352726278768,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "c0v8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1556074462,
      "version": 1541,
      "versionNonce": 1175511070,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766853283644,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*Phase flip oracle:\n\nThe oracles encodes a certain function f => We can use it to execute our search task via the function f                             \n                                                   => We need to define our search task with a function f\n\nWe're doing a search of a marked element over a list with N entries.\nIf we have a function f:L->{0,1} where: f(x)=1 if x is the searched element(s)  => We encoded our search task in this function!\n                                                 f(x)=0 otherwise\n\nGrover's algorithm can also be defined in this general form: for the given function f, it finds the element(s) x such that f(x)=1.\n\n\n                                            Travelling Salesman Problem: Starting in A, does there exist a path of distance < 900 which \n                                                                               visits all cities exactly once and returns to A? \n                                                                               \n                                                                               f(ABCDA)=1 ; f(others)=0\n\n\n\n\n\nWe'll use a particular type of oracles, the phase flip oracles, to execute the first part of our algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhen f(x)=1, a phase of (-1) is kicked back to the front of the first register =>we obtain a sign flip effect.\n\nNote that even if we don't know anything about f (that's why it is called a blackbox), we flipped the sign of the amplitude of \nthe searched element.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 44.32564362046029,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*Phase flip oracle:\n\nThe oracles encodes a certain function f => We can use it to execute our search task via the function f                             \n                                                   => We need to define our search task with a function f\n\nWe're doing a search of a marked element over a list with N entries.\nIf we have a function f:L->{0,1} where: f(x)=1 if x is the searched element(s)  => We encoded our search task in this function!\n                                                 f(x)=0 otherwise\n\nGrover's algorithm can also be defined in this general form: for the given function f, it finds the element(s) x such that f(x)=1.\n\n\n                                            Travelling Salesman Problem: Starting in A, does there exist a path of distance < 900 which \n                                                                               visits all cities exactly once and returns to A? \n                                                                               \n                                                                               f(ABCDA)=1 ; f(others)=0\n\n\n\n\n\nWe'll use a particular type of oracles, the phase flip oracles, to execute the first part of our algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhen f(x)=1, a phase of (-1) is kicked back to the front of the first register =>we obtain a sign flip effect.\n\nNote that even if we don't know anything about f (that's why it is called a blackbox), we flipped the sign of the amplitude of \nthe searched element.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
